# ADA perfection revision log

Whenever you perform a step that **refines ADA perfection** on this portfolio (theme `ada-first` / ADA Guy, high-contrast surfaces, assistive tech semantics, motion labeling, keyboard/focus, etc.), you must **append a new step** to the running log in:

[`src/data/content.ts`](src/data/content.ts) → `adaPerfectionMarkdown` (card **ADA perfection** under How this site works).

Do this in the **same change set** as the code fix when practical—not as a forgotten follow-up.

## Step shape (required)

Newest steps go **at the top** of the `# Revision steps` section (below the intro).

Each step **title** states the premise (the issue discovered). The body always has both subsections:

```markdown
## YYYY-MM-DD — <short premise / issue in the title>

### ADA refinement issue
What we found (user report, audit, or thoughtful scan). Why it matters for ADA Guy / WCAG.

### ADA refinement action
What we changed in the project (files, rules-engine flags, CSS, semantics). Link paths in backticks when helpful.
```

- **ADA refinement issue** = the problem / premise that motivated the work  
- **ADA refinement action** = the fulfillment we shipped (or “documented only” / “queued” if research-only)

Do **not** collapse issue and action into one paragraph. Do **not** invent fake fixes—if research-only, say so under **action**.

## Scope reminders

- Prefer rules-engine / theme gates for ADA-only behavior (`decorativeCardMedia`, swoop flags, tokens)—avoid hard-coding theme checks in many components when a layout rule already exists.
- Do **not** “fix” ADA by breaking other themes.
- Hero **video** behavior changes that affect both interactive and non-interactive modes should be called out explicitly in the step.
- After content edits that affect the site, follow `verify-build-before-resting` (build + static preview).
